    "result_text": "The build is green. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the city-capacity rule for `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is rejected with **409** when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners. I followed the existing \"reject\" pattern (mirroring `RejectDuplicateHousehold`) rather than modifying any existing function, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 every new function is a single, tiny cohesive unit and no existing method grew in complexity.\n\n**New files (each small, self-contained):**\n- `RejectCityAtCapacity.java` \u2014 counts existing owners whose city matches (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed) and throws `CityAtCapacityException` once the count reaches 50.\n- `CityAtCapacityException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n- `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds 409 (CONFLICT).\n- `...escalation.CityAtCapacityException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**One existing file touched:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `rejectCityAtCapacity` step (governed by the transaction) between `rejectDuplicateHousehold` and `build`.\n\nThe pre-existing acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a fresh city to exactly 50, expect the 51st to be a 409) passes, and the full suite is green.",
